  "title": "When a shared contact binds at the first step",
  "status": "Proved",
  "claim": "Two rings of at least three cells share one contact cell, each non-contact cell updating by its ring's elementary rule, so both rings read the contact; binding is read as in PROCESS-CONTACT-20261108-001A. (1) A contact that combines the rings' proposals, c(t + 1) = g(f_A(a_{m-1}, c, a_1), f_B(b_{n-1}, c, b_1)), binds both rings at lag 1 exactly when g is XOR or XNOR and neither rule is canalizing at its centre (f(., 0, .) and f(., 1, .) both nonconstant). (2) A self-blind contact, c(t + 1) = h(a_{m-1}, a_1, b_{n-1}, b_1), binds at lag 1 exactly when no setting of one ring's pair makes h constant in the other's; its parities u(a_{m-1}, a_1) + v(b_{n-1}, b_1) bind at lag 1 exactly when u and v are nonconstant. (3) With linear or affine rules and an affine contact law the shared system is affine, and the contact binds at lag T exactly when its row of M^T meets both rings.",
